A||||14||Mach10||Fiero Trek: The Trouble With Torque Converters||||5||
Z||000000||Mach10||07-05-2001||02:37 AM||||Captains log, stardate 050701.135:<BR>Ship cannot--Achieve--top speed... fast.. enough. Search for... upgraded torque... converter not going... well...<P>God, how did Shatner EVER manage to write any books?<P>Anyhoo:<BR>I am not having any luck finding a higher-stall torque converter for my Bunsen Burner. I've decided on keeping the Auto for now, simply because it's too much of a hassle to change to a 5spd, but I need a better launch. In the past, a slightly higher-stall converter was suggested, but I am not having any luck finding one. So, I'm asking to board a 2-part question:<BR>1) What numbers have been tried/tested/true for the 2.8? In terms of stall etc.<BR>2) Where the hell can I get a higher stall converter? I don't suppose there's a "stock" converter from another car entirely(that has a TH125) that has a better stall? Something nibbling at the back of my mind is saying Sunbird... Cannot remember where I saw that.<P>Oh, nevermind, the nibbling is my Iguana... Cool! He can talk  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyways, thanks in advance, guys/gals/doggies||||reg||5
Z||000001||84Bill||07-05-2001||08:58 AM||||Spock here.<BR>Captian I have prepared a shuttle for you in hangar bay 3, your destination is mainly (Romulan territory) 4 banger stuff but I think there is a few links for torque converters.  <A HREF="http://dukemods.50megs.com/WEB%20PAGE/PAGE1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://dukemods.50megs.com/WEB%20PAGE/PAGE1.html</A> <BR>Good luck Captian||||reg||14
Z||000002||Mach10||07-05-2001||11:10 AM||||Spock,<BR>I've combed that sector, found only a wormhole to the "angelfire" sector. That sector has been charted...<P>Actually, it was the angelfire/fierosails/info page that gave me the idea for a higher-stall converter. Trouble is, the manufacturers it lists don't seem to have anything but shift kits...<P>Still stumped.||||reg||7
Z||000003||AkursedX||07-05-2001||02:11 PM||||A converter from a Sunbird 1.8 should help you with your problem.  I don't know if it stalls higher though, but it does spin faster and is looser than the stock one.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||07-05-2001||02:16 PM||||DON'T make the mistake of putting in a 3.8 converter. You may as well tie a log to your fiero if you do ...slow slow slow<P><BR>The 1.8 converter works really well, it is looser as AkursedX said. The one car I have ridden in with a 1.8 converter ran up the rpm's quite a bit quicker than stock.<P><BR>you won't be sorry if you go to the 1.8 converter..works really nice, big difference. Not just shifting hard like the 3.8 so you think you are going faster<P>JM <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||07-05-2001||02:52 PM||||Do you have a model/year so I can comb the boneyards? This is EXACTLY what I'm looking for, guys! THANKS!<BR>HUGS AND KISSES FOR ALL!*<P>*You know, the chocolate kind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||Mach10||07-05-2001||04:45 PM||||*nudge*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||07-05-2001||04:55 PM||||Mach, go to your local Pontiac dealer parts guy.  Tell them you're contemplating the change and you want to check out the 'k-factors' on a few production torque converters.  They should be able to help you.  The higer the k-factor the more torque converting usually acomplished by a higher stall speed.  Pick the late 80's early 90's so that they are compatible with the fabulous THM-125 that Fieros use.  I would look at any 4-banger or small six that is a production 'performance' machine.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||07-05-2001||05:32 PM||||Found a couple. I have the option of a TqC from an 89 1.8 NA, or an '87 1.8 Turbo. I'd say the turbo, is that right, for whoever knows?||||reg||7
Z||000009||87GTZ34||07-05-2001||08:44 PM||||Mach,  As I mentioned ealier in the thread go (or call) your Pontiac dealer and they can look up the K-factor take the higher of the two.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dave Gunsul||07-05-2001||09:04 PM||||Captain, starfleet cmmand requests that you proceed immeadiately to these coordinates.<BR>Associated Auto Inc. Ph# (201)-457-9507 in<BR>NJ. Also email archie his trans guy makes<BR>a higher stall converter too. (if I remember<BR>correctly)<BR>This is a priority one mission.<BR>Dave, admiral, starfleet command, out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000011||Slammed Fiero||07-05-2001||09:24 PM||||A higher stall converter isn't really tuned to the torque band of a 2.8. This is a problem people run into when putting a 3.8 converter in a fiero. The 3.8 converter is designed around a motor with quite a bit more low end torque.<P>you can overstall a motor. Get the sunbird unit.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000012||grinthock||07-05-2001||09:55 PM||||Captain, Geordi LaForge here from Engineering, I have a report for you from someone who has done it before, we have confirmed that the Warp Engines are working at higher efficency now with the changes.<P><BR>I have to agree with Slammed I have a sunbird 1.8 Torque Conv in my 87 GT, VERY noticable difference in performance... Cost me $150 Canadian dollars for the unit and I already had the engine out so no install cost.<P>It runs great....  Definatly a good idea.<p>[This message has been edited by grinthock (edited 07-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dave Gunsul||07-05-2001||10:00 PM||||Slammed has a good point but if you've modified your engine enough it can use a higher stall than stock. <P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||07-06-2001||11:19 AM||||I know that it is very easy to "overstall" the engine to the point of uselessness, just as it's possible to understall it and lug it down. Frm what I gather, the stock converter is more economy/topend based than the sunbird, which because of the difference in torque probably will stall a little higher. <BR>I hear what yous guys are saying, and I will take it to heart. I'm not looking for a 3500rpm drag converter, rest assured  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I found a sunbird converter for cheap, so I'm gonna grab a used one, and have it rebuilt. I'll let you know how it turns out. Thanks for the help, guys!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
